{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At its core, React Query manages query caching for you based on query keys. Query keys can be as simple as a string, or as complex as an array of many strings and nested objects. As long as the query key is serializable, and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"unique to the query's data"}]},{"type":"text","value":", you can use it!"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"string-only-query-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"String-Only Query Keys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest form of a key is actually not an array, but an individual string. When a string query key is passed, it is converted to an array internally with the string as the only item in the query key. This format is useful for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generic List/Index resources"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-hierarchical resources"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// A list of todos\nuseQuery('todos', ...) // queryKey === ['todos']\n\n// Something else, whatever!\nuseQuery('somethingSpecial', ...) // queryKey === ['somethingSpecial']","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"array-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Array Keys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a query needs more information to uniquely describe its data, you can use an array with a string and any number of serializable objects to describe it. This is useful for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hierarchical or nested resources"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It's common to pass an ID, index, or other primitive to uniquely identify the item"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Queries with additional parameters"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It's common to pass an object of additional options"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// An individual todo\nuseQuery(['todo', 5], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todo', 5]\n\n// An individual todo in a \"preview\" format\nuseQuery(['todo', 5, { preview: true }], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todo', 5, { preview: true }]\n\n// A list of todos that are \"done\"\nuseQuery(['todos', { type: 'done' }], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todos', { type: 'done' }]","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"query-keys-are-hashed-deterministically","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Keys are hashed deterministically!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This means that no matter the order of keys in objects, all of the following queries are considered equal:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useQuery(['todos', { status, page }], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', { page, status }], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', { page, status, other: undefined }], ...)","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following query keys, however, are not equal. Array item order matters!"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useQuery(['todos', status, page], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', page, status], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', undefined, page, status], ...)","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"if-your-query-function-depends-on-a-variable-include-it-in-your-query-key","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your query function depends on a variable, include it in your query key"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since query keys uniquely describe the data they are fetching, they should include any variables you use in your query function that "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"change"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Todos({ todoId }) {\n  const result = useQuery(['todos', todoId], () => fetchTodoById(todoId))\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For tips on organizing Query Keys in larger applications, have a look at "},{"type":"link","href":"../community/tkdodos-blog#8-effective-react-query-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Effective React Query Keys"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from\nthe Community Resources."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"string-only-query-keys","text":"String-Only Query Keys","level":2},{"id":"array-keys","text":"Array Keys","level":2},{"id":"query-keys-are-hashed-deterministically","text":"Query Keys are hashed deterministically!","level":2},{"id":"if-your-query-function-depends-on-a-variable-include-it-in-your-query-key","text":"If your query function depends on a variable, include it in your query key","level":2},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nAt its core, React Query manages query caching for you based on query keys. Query keys can be as simple as a string, or as complex as an array of many strings and nested objects. As long as the query key is serializable, and **unique to the query's data**, you can use it!\n\n## String-Only Query Keys\n\nThe simplest form of a key is actually not an array, but an individual string. When a string query key is passed, it is converted to an array internally with the string as the only item in the query key. This format is useful for:\n\n- Generic List/Index resources\n- Non-hierarchical resources\n\n```js\n// A list of todos\nuseQuery('todos', ...) // queryKey === ['todos']\n\n// Something else, whatever!\nuseQuery('somethingSpecial', ...) // queryKey === ['somethingSpecial']\n```\n\n## Array Keys\n\nWhen a query needs more information to uniquely describe its data, you can use an array with a string and any number of serializable objects to describe it. This is useful for:\n\n- Hierarchical or nested resources\n  - It's common to pass an ID, index, or other primitive to uniquely identify the item\n- Queries with additional parameters\n  - It's common to pass an object of additional options\n\n```js\n// An individual todo\nuseQuery(['todo', 5], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todo', 5]\n\n// An individual todo in a \"preview\" format\nuseQuery(['todo', 5, { preview: true }], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todo', 5, { preview: true }]\n\n// A list of todos that are \"done\"\nuseQuery(['todos', { type: 'done' }], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todos', { type: 'done' }]\n```\n\n## Query Keys are hashed deterministically!\n\nThis means that no matter the order of keys in objects, all of the following queries are considered equal:\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos', { status, page }], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', { page, status }], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', { page, status, other: undefined }], ...)\n```\n\nThe following query keys, however, are not equal. Array item order matters!\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos', status, page], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', page, status], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', undefined, page, status], ...)\n```\n\n## If your query function depends on a variable, include it in your query key\n\nSince query keys uniquely describe the data they are fetching, they should include any variables you use in your query function that **change**. For example:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos({ todoId }) {\n  const result = useQuery(['todos', todoId], () => fetchTodoById(todoId))\n}\n```\n\n## Further reading\n\nFor tips on organizing Query Keys in larger applications, have a look at [Effective React Query Keys](../community/tkdodos-blog#8-effective-react-query-keys) from\nthe Community Resources.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: query-keys\ntitle: Query Keys\n---\n\nAt its core, React Query manages query caching for you based on query keys. Query keys can be as simple as a string, or as complex as an array of many strings and nested objects. As long as the query key is serializable, and **unique to the query's data**, you can use it!\n\n## String-Only Query Keys\n\nThe simplest form of a key is actually not an array, but an individual string. When a string query key is passed, it is converted to an array internally with the string as the only item in the query key. This format is useful for:\n\n- Generic List/Index resources\n- Non-hierarchical resources\n\n```js\n// A list of todos\nuseQuery('todos', ...) // queryKey === ['todos']\n\n// Something else, whatever!\nuseQuery('somethingSpecial', ...) // queryKey === ['somethingSpecial']\n```\n\n## Array Keys\n\nWhen a query needs more information to uniquely describe its data, you can use an array with a string and any number of serializable objects to describe it. This is useful for:\n\n- Hierarchical or nested resources\n  - It's common to pass an ID, index, or other primitive to uniquely identify the item\n- Queries with additional parameters\n  - It's common to pass an object of additional options\n\n```js\n// An individual todo\nuseQuery(['todo', 5], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todo', 5]\n\n// An individual todo in a \"preview\" format\nuseQuery(['todo', 5, { preview: true }], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todo', 5, { preview: true }]\n\n// A list of todos that are \"done\"\nuseQuery(['todos', { type: 'done' }], ...)\n// queryKey === ['todos', { type: 'done' }]\n```\n\n## Query Keys are hashed deterministically!\n\nThis means that no matter the order of keys in objects, all of the following queries are considered equal:\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos', { status, page }], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', { page, status }], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', { page, status, other: undefined }], ...)\n```\n\nThe following query keys, however, are not equal. Array item order matters!\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos', status, page], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', page, status], ...)\nuseQuery(['todos', undefined, page, status], ...)\n```\n\n## If your query function depends on a variable, include it in your query key\n\nSince query keys uniquely describe the data they are fetching, they should include any variables you use in your query function that **change**. For example:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos({ todoId }) {\n  const result = useQuery(['todos', todoId], () => fetchTodoById(todoId))\n}\n```\n\n## Further reading\n\nFor tips on organizing Query Keys in larger applications, have a look at [Effective React Query Keys](../community/tkdodos-blog#8-effective-react-query-keys) from\nthe Community Resources.\n"}
